N2  - Gestational weight gain leads to several complications during pregnancy and the postpartum period. More than 60% of pregnant women exceed the weight gain goal, resulting in postpartum weight retention and difficulties during delivery in caesarean. Recent research has shown the significant impact of gut microorganisms on the metabolic alterations that affect the physiology of pregnant women, leading to the development of metabolic disorders such as gestational diabetes mellitus, gestational obesity, various cardiovascular diseases, and non-alcoholic liver diseases. The makeup of the gut microbiota undergoes modification throughout both the first and third trimesters of pregnancy. The diet consumed during this era undergoes significant modifications, leading to the development of dysbiosis. While the alteration of gut microorganisms is often transitory and returns to its former condition over time, in some instances it might result in enduring metabolic patterns. The levels of Akkermansia, Bifidobacterium, and Firmicutes have increased, and these bacteria are linked to an increased need for storing energy. This study discusses the correlation of diet and demographic factors with microbial changes during pregnancy resulting in gestational obesity and other related complications in women in Northeast India.
