N2  - Introduction: Nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH), also known as MASH, and metabolic syndrome (MetS) are associated with considerable morbidity and healthcare costs. However, little is known about the incremental cost of each condition to the other. The aim of this study is to quantify the incremental costs of patients with NASH and MetS. Methods: Adults 18+ years at index with ≥1 diagnosis code for NASH, and/or ≥3 of 4 possible MetS conditions (T2D, Hypertension, Obesity, Hyperlipidemia/Hypercholesterolemia) within 1 year of each other, were identified in the Veradigm Integrated Dataset using electronic health records or claims, between 7/1/2018 and 8/31/2022. Patients with viral hepatitis, alcohol-use disorder or alcoholic liver disease, opioid disorders, cirrhosis, type 1 diabetes, gestational diabetes, or bariatric surgery were excluded. Patients had to have ≥12 months of continuous claims enrollment before (baseline) and after (follow-up) the index date. The index date was the latest NASH diagnosis or date when the 3rd MetS criteria was met. Patients were stratified into 3 cohorts: NASH-only, MetS-only, and NASH+MetS. Baseline patient characteristics and healthcare costs were described. We fit a generalized linear model using follow-up costs by excluding outliers (costs in top 1%), then controlling for disease cohort, age, sex, a modified Charlson comorbidity index (mCCI), and flag for index year of 2022, to estimate the per year all-cause healthcare costs and incremental cost of adding MetS to a NASH diagnosis (or vice versa). Incremental costs were estimated by changing from NASH-only or MetSonly to NASH+MetS and observing the change in cost. Results: We identified 37,850 patients with NASH-only, 1,783,918 patients with MetS-only, and 18,906 patients with NASH+MetS. Mean (SD) age was 52.2 (14.7), 61.2 (13.5), and 57.8 (12.3) years for the NASH-only, MetS-only, and NASH+MetS cohorts, respectively. During the baseline, the NASH+MetS cohort had a higher mean (SD) mCCI score of 0.8 (1.3) compared to 0.5 (1.0) for NASH-only and 0.7 (1.2) for MetS-only. 9.0% of the NASH-only cohort had an inpatient admission during follow-up, with mean inpatient costs of $3,533, compared to 14.3% of the MetSonly cohort ($5,845) and 12.0% of the NASH+MetS cohort ($5,773). 25.7%, 27.3%, and 30.7% of the NASH-only, MetS-only, and NASH+MetS cohorts respectively had an emergency department visit during follow-up. The model-predicted mean total cost during follow-up was $12,563 for patients with NASH-only, $14,673 for patients with MetS-only, and $17,818 for patients with NASH+MetS. The incremental increase in costs of adding MetS to NASH was 10.1% (+$1,275). The incremental increase in costs of adding NASH to MetS was 15.1% (+$2,210). Conclusion: Both NASH and MetS contribute to the high healthcare costs among patients with a dual diagnosis of NASH+MetS. Results from our analysis imply that NASH comprises a higher portion of total healthcare costs among patients with NASH and MetS .
