N2  - obesity impacts multiple aspects of health. it is closely related to a number of adverse reproductive outcomes, including anovulation, polycystic ovary syndrome, and infertility. a correlation between obesity/dysmetabolism and oocyte maturation pathways, endometrial characteristics, and embryonic quality has been demonstrated. obesity, by increasing the risk of metabolic disorders such as insulin resistance, diabetes, or relative hyperandrogenism, also adversely affects reproductive function, causing a chronic inflammatory state that is reflected both systemically and locally, at the ovarian follicular microenvironment, altering oocyte maturation and quality. obesity comes to affect endometrial receptivity, with lower clinical pregnancy and live birth rates and higher abortion rates. it also appears to have a negative impact on assisted reproduction outcomes, with fewer implantations and clinical pregnancies, higher incidence of miscarriage or complications during gestation. obese women are more likely to create poor-quality embryos. Whether these results are due to defects in oocyte quality, abnormal embryo development, or an unwelcoming uterine environment is still debated, but clinical evidence to date suggests a combination of factors. A healthy diet, combined with sufficient antioxidant intake, body weight control and regular physical activity, is estimated to reduce the risk of ovulatory infertility. Medications to treat obesity may be useful in combination with lifestyle interventions; bariatric surgery remains the most effective intervention for significant and lasting weight loss. These interventions improve quality of life and may increase the likelihood of spontaneous conception. Further studies are needed to define the impact of obesity in the short and long term, from pregnancy research to pregnancy success.
