N2  - Introduction: Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) has become the leading cause of liver disease and cirrhosis in USA, with potential complications for productive-aged women. The studies on NAFLD in pregnant women are limited. We aimed in our study to evaluate the effect of NAFLD on pregnancy outcomes using a large database. Methods: We performed a case control study with the National Inpatient Sample (NIS) using ICD10-CM and PCS codes to identify patients with pregnancy and patients with history of NAFLD. Primary outcomes were differences in mortality rate and rates of inpatient outcomes and complications between pregnant patients with NAFLD and those without(controls). For categorical variables we used Chi-squred test with Rao and Scott's second order correction. For Continuous variables we used Wilcoxon rank-sum test for complex survey samples. All analysis were performed using R studio. Results: Out of a total of 12,039,222 pregnant women in the cohort, 7,315 (0.06%) had history of NAFLD. These patients were older (30.6 vs 28.7; p<0.001) and more likely to be Hispanic or white (41% and 38%), respectively. Table 1 summarizes the demographic characteristics of the cohort. Comparing to pregnant women without NAFLD, patients with NAFLD had higher prevalence of major antepartum complications like pre-eclampsia (21% vs 5.4%) and gestational diabetes (24% vs 7.4%), both p<0.001. In addition, NAFLD cohort are more likely to undergo induction of labor (32% vs 24%; p< 0.001), or cesarean delivery (38% vs 23%; p<0.001). Moreover, severe maternal morbidity (SMM) conditions are increased with NAFLD like shock (0.5% vs <0.1%), ARDS (0.9% vs 0.1%), DIC (4.2% vs 0.2%), acute renal failure (5.5% vs 0.2%) and postpartum hemorrhage (12% vs 5.1%), all p<0.001. NAFLD group had more fetal complications like pre-term birth (12% vs 5.7%’ p<0.001), Intrauterine Growth Restriction (IUGR) (4.9% vs 3.3% p<0.001), fetal distress (34% vs 22%; p<0.001) and stillbirth (0.3% vs <0.1% p = 0.026), table 2. Conclusion: Our study concludes that NAFLD is significantly associated with obstetric adverse events, severe maternal morbidity and major fetal complications. With NAFLD prevalence on the rise, it should be considered a high-risk condition in pregnancy and Maternal-Fetal medicine care is recommended.
