N2  - Introduction: Maternal fat accumulation increases in the first half of pregnancy because of increased insulin resistance, increased lipid synthesis, and maternal hyperlipidemia. Pregnancy-induced hyperlipidemia itself can cause fat accumulation in the liver, which is highly sensitive to metabolic alternation. Although previous studies have shown that non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) that predates pregnancy is associated with adverse pregnancy outcomes, there are no studies investigating pregnancy outcomes in women who develop NAFLD during pregnancy. Since NAFLD/hepatic insulin resistance can result in exaggerated fetal growth from increased glucose and fatty acid release, we examined whether new-onset NAFLD during pregnancy was associated with excessive fetal growth. Methods: This is a secondary analysis of a large prospective cohort study of healthy Korean women designed to examine pregnancy outcome in the setting of NAFLD. Metabolic profiling and liver ultrasounds were performed to diagnose NAFLD in the first and late second trimester (24-28 weeks). Based on these results, the study population was divided into three groups: Group 1, no NAFLD in both first and second trimester; Group 2, no NAFLD in first trimester but new-onset NAFLD in second trimester; Group 3, NAFLD in both first and second trimester. Large-for-gestational age (LGA) was defined as birthweight >90 percentile for gestational age. Results: A total of 1474 women were included in the analysis, including 1210 with no NAFLD (Group 1), 69 with new-onset NAFLD (Group 2), and 195 with NAFLD in both trimesters (Group 3). Compared with Group 1, the risk of LGA was higher in Group 3 as expected but also in Group 2. The increased risk in Group 2 remained signifcant after adjustment for multiple variables, including gestational diabetes and pre-pregnancy BMI (aOR, 2.27; 95% CI, 1.05-4.44). Conclusion: New-onset NAFLD during pregnancy was associated with a two-fold increased risk of delivering an LGA infant, suggesting that excessive fat accumulation in the liver during pregnancy may be a marker of or directly afect fetal growth.
