N2  - Polycystic ovary syndrome is closely related to complex multisystem conditions such as metabolic, endocrine, psychological, fertility, and pregnancy-related implications at all stages of life. The majority of women with PCOS manifest several metabolic features including obesity, insulin resistance (IR), dyslipidemia, and hyperandrogenism. PCOS results in an increased risk of developing metabolic disease such as type 2 diabetes, non- alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD), cardiovascular disease; various pregnancy complications such as preeclampsia, gestational diabetes, macrosomia, growth restriction, miscarriage, stillbirth, and premature delivery. PCOS affects more than 10% of women of reproductive age, an estimated more than 200 million women worldwide. PCOS is thought to be increasing in incidence in both developing and developed countries as a result of lifestyle-related changes in diet quality, reduced physical activity, endocrine-disrupting chemicals (EDCs), changes in light exposure, sleep disturbances, increased stress levels, and others. Lifestyle influencing changes in the character of the gastrointestinal microbiome is another important factor in the etiology of PCOS. Several studies have reported reduced alpha diversity of the microbiome which has been associated with metabolic, endocrine, and clinical features observed in women with PCOS. The resulting dysbiosis has been shown to be reversible after interventions aimed at improving diet or treatment with probiotics or symbiotic. In our observations, we found that inadequate daily fiber intake, lack of intake of several micronutrients such as vitamin D, B6, niacin and folic acid had a significant relationship with the incidence of PCOS in Jakarta.
