N2  - Background & Aims: Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is a hepatic manifestation of metabolic disease and has been associated with adverse pregnancy outcome such as gestational diabetes, macrosomia, pregnancyassociated hypertension. Intrauterine exposure to unfavorable metabolic environments, such as insulin resistance and dyslipidemia, is related with less favorable metabolic profile of offspring later in life. However, there is paucity of information whether this relationship is originated from fetal metabolic alteration. In this study, we attempted to investigate the impact of NAFLD on neonatal lipid profile at birth. Methods: The current study is a secondary analysis of a prospective cohort designed to examine the risk of NAFLD during pregnancy. The study population consisted of 43 pregnant women with NAFLD and 172 normal pregnant women without NAFLD, selected after propensity scoring matching for maternal age, parity, maternal BMI, gestational diabetes, and pregnancyassociated hypertension. We compared fetal metabolic laboratory result between offspring born to mothers with NAFLD and without NAFLD. Results: The concentration of fetal HDL was significantly lower in offspring born to mothers with NAFLD. The risk of HDL dyslipidemia increased significantly in offspring born to mothers and HDL dyslipidemia remained significant after adjustment for gestational age at delivery, birthweight, gestational diabetes, and maternal BMI. (Adjusted odds ratio, 6.12; 95% CI, 1.11-33.68). Conclusions: Intrauterine exposure to maternal NAFLD was associated with the risk of HDL dyslipidemia, suggesting fetal metabolic alteration in pregnant women with NAFLD.
