N2  - Background: Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) has been shown to be associated with adverse outcomes during pregnancy. It remains unclear if this association is solely related to NAFLD versus a reflection of the systemic metabolic derangements associated with NAFLD. In addition, FIB-4 is a validated predictive model for non-invasive fibrosis assessment. Our study examines if NAFLD and its severity, based on FIB-4 score, have independent associations with worse maternal and fetal outcomes. Methods: This is a retrospective study of all adult pregnant patients who received care at a North American tertiary center between 2000 and 2021. ICD codes and keywords from radiology reports were used to identify patients with NAFLD prior to pregnancy to compare to controls with no prior history of any liver disease. We initially matched controls to cases in a 2:1 manner by age within 3years of pregnancy. Patients without AST, ALT, and platelet values obtained within the 2years prior to estimated date of conception were then excluded. Baseline FIB-4 score was calculated to determine pre-pregnancy NAFLD severity. The outcomes of interest were gestational age, birthweight, pre-eclampsia, 5-minute APGAR scores, gestational diabetes, and gestational hypertension. Multivariate linear regression and logistic regression were used to study the impact of severity of NAFLD on maternal and fetal outcomes. Results: A total of 290 patients were included with 119 NAFLD cases and 171 controls. Patients with NAFLD had statistically higher rates of underlying comorbidities including diabetes mellitus (NAFLD 27.7% vs control 9.9%, p <0.001), hypertension (23.5% vs.10.5%, p = 0.005), obesity (55.5% vs. 30.4% p <0.001), and metabolic syndrome (16.8% vs 4.7%, p = 0.001). Comparing pregnant women with NAFLD to controls, there were no statistically significant associations within maternal or fetal outcomes on univariate analysis. When the NAFLD group was stratified by severity of underlying fibrosis using prepregnancy FIB-4 score, there was no statistically significant association between higher FIB-4 score and adverse maternal and fetal outcomes in multivariate regression analysis (Table 1). Conclusion: NAFLD and its severity, assessed by FIB4, do not appear to have independent associations with adverse maternal and fetal outcomes. These outcomes are independently associated with extra-hepatic components of metabolic syndrome. Multicenter prospective cohort studies are needed to validate our findings.
