N2  - Background: Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is the most common liver disease in the United States, affecting 10-15% of pregnant women. Emerging data suggest that infants born to mothers with NAFLD have higher risk of adverse neonatal outcomes, such as preterm birth and large size for gestational age. However, data on long-term outcomes are limited. We evaluated infants born to mothers with (and without) NAFLD in pregnancy throughout the first year of life. Methods: Subjects were identified through an ongoing prospective study of pregnant women with and without NAFLD. Electronic health records for neonatal, six month, and one year outcomes were reviewed. If pediatric care continued outside the health system, phone consent was obtained from mothers to collect data. The primary outcome was growth in the first year of life, defined as weight and weight percentiles at birth, six months, and one year. Secondary outcomes included APGAR score, NICU admission, neonatal complications, and achievement of developmental milestones at six months and one year. Outcomes in infants of mothers with NAFLD were compared to infants of mothers without NAFLD. Results: We identified 536 infants born to mothers who were assessed for NAFLD on liver ultrasound. We obtained neonatal data for 486 infants (73 [15%] maternal NAFLD), six month data for 202 infants (32 [16%] maternal NAFLD), and one year data for 155 infants (25 [16%] maternal NAFLD). Maternal NAFLD was positively associated with maternal pre-pregnancy BMI > 25 (p<0.001), increased BMI at delivery (p<0.001), pre-pregnancy diabetes mellitus (p<0.001), and gestational diabetes mellitus (p=0.024). Maternal NAFLD was not associated with a significant difference in gestational age at delivery (271 vs 273 days, p=0.143), birth weight (3.15 vs 3.18 kg, p= 0.192) or weight-for-gestational-age percentile (50 vs 42, p=0.133). Maternal NAFLD was also not associated with a difference in weight or weight-for-length percentile at six months (7.79 vs 8.05 kg, p=0.576; 39.4 vs 36.85 %, p=0.503) or one year (9.66 vs 10.4 kg, p= 0.340; 47.6 vs 54.8 %, p=0.424). Maternal NAFLD was associated with a lower rate of developmental delays (p=0.027), particularly motor delays (p=0.024), at six months, but not at one year. Conclusions: Though we identified important metabolic differences in mothers with and without NAFLD, the presence of maternal NAFLD did not adversely affect gestational age at delivery, birth weight, or infant growth during the first year of life. Further research is needed to determine whether maternal NAFLD affects offspring obesity and development later in childhood, as well as to investigate if subgroup analyses of the NAFLD cohort (i.e. those with higher BMIs or advanced liver disease) are associated with adverse neonatal/pediatric outcomes.
