N2  - Background: Emerging data suggests that NAFLD is independently associated with adverse outcomes of pregnancy (AOPs), including gestational diabetes (GDM) and preeclampsia (PE). However, the prevalence of NAFLD-associated gene variants in patients with AOPs has not been evaluated. Methods: The BioMe Biobank, which contains exome sequence data linked with electronic health record (EHR) data from over 30,000 patients, was used to retrospectively identify women with NAFLD, GDM and PE based on ICD9/ICD10 codes. Cases were validated by chart review. The minor allele frequency (MAF) of four NAFLDassociated gene variants, including TM6SF2 rs58542926, HSD17B13 rs72613567, PNPLA3 I148M rs738409 and MBOAT7 rs641738, was determined for each diagnosis group of interest and for disease-free women. Further analysis of the gene variants in women with the diagnoses of interest and ALT ≥25 or ALT ≥50 was performed. Contingency tables assessed the prevalence of carriers and non-carriers of variants of interest. Differences between groups were identified using chi (X)-squared analysis. Results: Among 18,745 women included in BioMe, 628 (3.35%) patients with NAFLD, 238 (1.27%) patients with GDM, 153 (0.82%) patients with PE, and 17,759 disease-free females were identified. MAFs for the variants of interest are shown in Table 1. Women with NAFLD exhibited the highest MAFs for TM6FS2, PNPLA3 and MBOAT7 variants compared to all other groups. The PE group showed the lowest MAF for the HSD17B13 variant. The GDM group had an elevated MAF for the PNPLA3 variant compared to non-NAFLD subgroups and the highest MAF for the HSD17B13 variant. Relative prevalence of carriers of variants of interest in diagnosis groups of interest are presented in Table 2. Although the PNPLA3 variant was present at significantly higher frequency in females with NAFLD compared to those without NAFLD (p-value <0.001), the prevalence of the TM6FS2, MBOAT7 and HS17B13 variants was not significantly different among women with NAFLD compared to those without NAFLD. There were no statistically significant differences in NAFLD-associated variants in PE and GDM compared to disease-free women. Conclusions: Although we identified numeric differences in MAFs for the HSD17B13 variant in women with PE, and for PNPLA3 and HSD17B13 variants in women with GDM, we did not detect statistically significant differences in NAFLDassociated gene frequencies, suggesting either lack of association or insufficient sample sizes. The elevated MAFs for TM6FS2, PNPLA3, and MBOAT7 variants in the NAFLD group, as well as the statistically significant difference in PNPLA3 carrier frequency in women with NAFLD, confirms previously validated findings. Larger studies may be needed to further investigate carrier frequency of NAFLD-associated genes and differences in the prevalence of variants among women with AOPs. (Table Presented)
