N2  - Objective: Preeclampsia is a multisystem disorder of unknown cause that is unique to human gestation, and is known to be characterized by abnormal vascular response to placentation, associated with increased systemic vascular resistance. Liver involvement as one of the main clinical severe features of preeclampsia, led us to examine a possible association between chronic liver diseases and preeclampsia. Study Design: A retrospective analysis of 311 women with preexisting chronic liver disease (study group), including viral and autoimmune hepatitis, non-alcoholic fatty liver, Wilson's disease, and cirrhosis, compared to 933 healthy women (control group) matched by age, parity, and number of fetuses. Primary outcome was defined as the incidence of preeclampsia in each group. Secondary outcomes were obstetrical and neonatal complications. Results: Twenty-eight (9%) women in the study group and thirty-three (3.54%) women in the control group were diagnosed with preeclampsia (Odds ratio [OR] 2.69, 95% Confidence interval [CI] 1.603-4.544). Using a multivariate analysis adjusted for maternal age, parity, previous preeclampsia, chronic hypertension, gestational diabetes mellitus, pregestational diabetes mellitus, antiphospholipid syndrome, and mode of conception, we found that chronic liver disease was an independent risk factor for preeclampsia (adjusted OR 2.631, 95% CI 1.518-4.561). Women in the study group had a higher incidence of gestational diabetes mellitus (18.01% vs. 9.11%, p< 0.001), and a lower mean newborn weight (3088±551.80 grams vs. 3182.41±grams, p=0.011) as well. All other parameters did not defer between the groups. Conclusion: In our cohort, preexisting chronic liver disease was associated with a 2.6-fold increased risk for preeclampsia.
