N2  - Background: Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is strongly associated with type-2 diabetes mellitus. NAFLD in pregnant women may predispose them to gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM). Aims: We studied the prevalence of NAFLD and its association with GDM in southern Indian pregnant women. We also assessed the relationship of serum adiponectin level and Homeostatic Model Assessment of Insulin Resistance (HOMA-IR) value with NAFLD in pregnancy. Methods: Consecutive primigravida women in the first trimester attending our obstetric clinic between August 2019 and March 2021 were screened for NAFLD using liver ultrasound. Those with pre-existing diabetes, chronic liver disease, multiple pregnancy, or a history of alcohol consumption were excluded. Two groups with (group 1) and without (group 2) evidence of NAFLD had serum adiponectin and HOMA-IR estimation in the first trimester, and glucose-tolerance-test for GDM at 24-28 weeks of gestation. The association between NAFLD in pregnancy and the development of GDM was assessed by multiple logistic regression. Results: Of the 504 women screened, 23.1% (n=116; mild 83, moderate 33, none severe) had NAFLD. During their follow up, 24.5% in group 1(24 out of 98) and 13.2% in group 2 (25 out of 190), developed GDM (odds ratio=2.14; P=0.015). The risk increased with the severity of NAFLD (mild:13/72, moderate:11/26; P=0.018). Women with NAFLD had higher HOMA-IR than those without NAFLD, but the two groups had similar serum adiponectin levels. On multivariable logistic regression, moderate NAFLD was an independent risk factor for GDM, after adjusting for age, family history of DM, BMI and mean blood pressure (adjusted odds ratio=3.08; P=0.023). Conclusion: NAFLD is common during the first trimester of pregnancy, and its occurrence, particularly in higher grades, is an independent risk factor for the development of GDM.
