N2  - Background: Maternal and fetal outcomes in pregnant patients with NAFLD has been largely unexplored. To determine the level of evidence associated with both maternal and fetal outcomes in pregnant women with NAFLD. Methods: We conducted a comprehensive literature search. The studies included pregnant patients with a previous, current or subsequent diagnosis of NAFLD. A MeSH term search was conducted using topic search of NAFLD, Pregnancy, maternal and fetal outcomes as well as using a keyword search via Medline, Embase and Cochrane Library. We used a randomeffects model using odds ratios (OR) with 95% confidence intervals (CI). Results: Fourteen studies, with 12377 female NAFLD patients were reviewed. The results highlight that NAFLD patients had a statistically significant increased likelihood of pre-pregnancy Diabetes Mellitus (OR= 8.87, 95% CI: 3.98-19.78; P<0.001, n=4, I2=83.42%), baseline Hypertension (OR: 4.76, 95% CI: 2.97-7.62; p<0.001, n=3, I2=36.73%), Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (OR=3.06, 95% CI:1.74-5.39, P<0.001, n=5, I2=83.50%), Gestational Hypertension (OR=1.83, 95% CI:1.03-3.26, P=0.041, n=2, I2=43.81%), and Pre-eclampsia (OR=2.43, 95% CI:1.46-4.04; P=0.001, n=3, I2=0.00%). The analysis of the literature also revealed that pregnant NAFLD patients were significantly more likely to have a premature birth (OR=1.87, 95% CI: 1.50-2.33; P<0.001, n=4, I2=21.03%), large for gestational age (LGA) birth (OR=2.06, 95% CI: 1.83-2.31; P<0.001, n=1) or a history of prior miscarriage or abortion (OR: 1.15, 95% CI: 1.02-1.30, P=0.02, n=2, I2=0.00%). Egger's regression revealed no evidence of publication bias (p>0.05). Conclusion: This meta-analysis provides pooled evidence that NAFLD is associated with a substantial increase in maternal diabetic and hypertensive complications and that mothers with NAFLD are more likely to have premature births, a LGA birth and a history of prior miscarriage or abortion compared to normal controls. This data is important for clinicians managing these patients before, during and after pregnancy.
