N2  - Background and Aim: Maternal and fetal outcomes in pregnant patients with non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) have been largely unexplored. We aimed to determine the level of evidence associated with both maternal and fetal outcomes in pregnant women with NAFLD. Methods: We conducted a comprehensive literature search. The studies included pregnant patients with a previous, current, or subsequent diagnosis of NAFLD. We used a random-effects model using odds ratios (ORs) with 95% confidence intervals. Results: Fourteen studies, with 12 377 female patients with NAFLD, were reviewed. Patients with NAFLD had a statistically significantly increased likelihood of baseline diabetes mellitus (OR, 5.79; 95% CI, 1.23-27.36; P = 0.027; I2 = 84.85, P = 0.01; n = 4), baseline hypertension (OR, 4.76; 95% CI, 2.97-7.62; P < 0.001, n = 3), gestational diabetes mellitus (OR, 3.06; 95% CI, 1.74-5.39; P < 0.001; I2 = 83.50, P = 0.002; n = 5), gestational hypertension (OR, 1.83; 95% CI, 1.03-3.26; P = 0.041; I2 = 43.81, P = 0.18; n = 2), and pre-eclampsia (OR, 2.43; 95% CI, 1.46-4.04; P = 0.001; I2 = 0.00, P = 0.94; n = 3). The analysis of the literature also showed that pregnant patients with NAFLD were significantly more likely to have a premature birth (OR, 1.87; 95% CI, 1.50-2.33; P < 0.001; I2 = 21.03, P = 0.28; n = 4), large-for-gestational-age birth (OR, 2.06; 95% CI, 1.83-2.31; P < 0.001, I2 = 0.00, P = 1.00; n = 1), or a history of prior miscarriage or abortion (OR, 1.15; 95% CI, 1.02-1.30; P = 0.02, n = 2). Egger's regression showed no evidence of publication bias (P > 0.05). Conclusion: This meta-analysis provides pooled evidence that NAFLD is associated with a substantial increase in maternal diabetic and hypertensive complications and multiple adverse fetal outcomes. These data are important for clinicians managing these patients before, during, and after pregnancy.
