N2  - Background] The frequency of gestational diabetes insipidus (DI) has been reported to be 2~4/100,000 pregnancies. Acute fatty liver of pregnancya AFLP) is also more likely to be encoun-tered in these cases. Since both are rare diseases, this relation-ship may be easily missed.. Case] A 30-year-old 3 gravida 2 para Japanese woman was referred to our hospital at 36 weeks of ges-tation for management of dichorionic diamniotica DD) twin preg-nancy. Despite an uneventful first 2 trimesters, the patient had become aware of polyuria and general fatigue at 33 weeks of gestation, which gradually progressed for over 2 weeks. She was hospitalized for severe dehydration and mild liver dysfunction. Treatment for dehydration was started. On the day after admis-sion, polyuria of 4,300 mL/day, very low urine osmolalitya 75 Osm/L) , and hepatorenal deterioration were observed. Consid-ering the possibility of transient secondary DI from altered vasopressinase clearance because of hepatic dysfunction, we im-mediately performed an emergency cesarian section. A few hours postoperatively, the patient showed coagulopathy and ex-acerbation of hepatorenal function, and we diagnosed AFLP as 6 of the Swansea criteria were satisfied. Coagulopathy and hepatorenal function improved within a few days, but urine volume did not decrease. Oral medicine of desmopressin was used, and currently, outpatient treatment for central DI is being contin-ued.. Conclusion] While evaluating polyuria during pregnancy, we should consider the existence of critical conditions associ-ated with liver dysfunction, such as AFLP. Furthermore, it is important that these patients receive proper intensive care and appropriate judgment for termination of pregnancy.
