N2  - Background: Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is the leading cause of chronic liver disease in youth worldwide. Little is known of dietary patterns that may protect against or increase risk of NAFLD in children and adolescents. Objectives: (1) To characterize dietary patterns in a cohort of multi-ethnic youth in Colorado at two time-points: age 6-14 years (T1) and 12-19 years (T2); (2) to identify dietary patterns associated with hepatic fat fraction (HFF) at T2; (3) to examine associations of change in the dietary patterns with HFF among 397 youth in the Exploring Perinatal Outcomes in CHildren (EPOCH) Study. Methods: We used principal components analysis to derive dietary patterns from food frequency questionnaire data collected at T1 and T2, and measured HFF via magnetic resonance imaging at T2. We examined associations of dietary patterns with natural log-( ln)-transformed HFF using multivariable linear regression. Covariates included maternal education, gestational diabetes, and smoking habits; and child's pubertal status, body mass index, and physical activity levels. Results: At T1, we identified three dietary patterns, none of which were associated with HFF. At T2, we identified a Prudent and a Western dietary pattern. The Prudent pattern, characterized by high fruit and vegetable intake, was inversely associated with HFF (-0.08 [95% CI: -0.16, -0.00] units ln-HFF) whereas the Western pattern, comprising fried foods and refined carbohydrates, correlated with higher HFF among non-Hispanic White participants only (0.16 [0.06, 0.26] units ln-HFF). An increase in adherence to the Prudent pattern across T1 and T2 corresponded with 0.11 (0.04, 0.18) units lower ln-HFF. These findings persisted after accounting for concurrent BMI. Conclusions: A diet high in fruits and vegetables may be protective against fatty liver disease in youth, whereas greater intake of fried foods and refined carbohydrates is related to higher liver fat content in non-Hispanic White adolescents.
