N2  - Introduction: Acute fatty liver in pregnancy (AFLP) is a rare obstetric emergency characterised by maternal liver dysfunction and micro vesicular fatty infiltration of hepatocytes that may lead to significant maternal and perinatal morbidity and mortality. The diagnosis of AFLP is usually made clinically, with an overlap between AFLP, HELLP syndrome and pre-eclampsia (PET). Case presentation: A 42-year-old G4P2 with no significant past history presented at 35/40 gestation with absent fetal movements and a background of two weeks of polydipsia (5-6 L of water/24 hr), polyuria, fatigue and intermittent mild headache. She was normotensive at regular antenatal reviews. On presentation an intrauterine fetal death was confirmed and a diagnosis of PET/HELLP syndrome was made on the basis of BP 140/100 mmHg urine PCR 73, platelets 109 × 109/L, creatinine 194 μmol/L and LFT derangement. MgSo4 was commenced and emergency LUSC performed. Post-operatively, evolving biochemical derangement including ALT 674 U/L, AST 540 U/L, bilirubin 39 μmol/L, BSL 3.4 mmol/L, INR 1.3 and elevated APTT suggested diagnosis of AFLP. She was admitted to ICU with grade 2 encephalopathy and acute kidney injury and managed supportively. She improved biochemically and clinically and discharged Day 7. Discussion: Mild polydipsia and polyuria is often considered normal in pregnancy and not routinely investigated. Gestational diabetes insipidus (GDI) is a rare endocrinopathy with an incidence of 4 per 100,000 pregnancies and may be associated with AFLP or PET as demonstrated in our case. Increased awareness for maternity care providers may prompt earlier detection of evolving AFLP and therefore possible prevention of significant maternal and perinatal morbidity and mortality.
