N2  - Objective: Early prediction of gestational diabetes (GDM) can help to develop preventive strategies, and guidelines traditionally recommended high-risk factors [Conventional risk factors in ACOG]. In 2017, the ACOG recommended new criteria, but not universally adopted [New risk factors in ACOG]. Recently, nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) was suggested as a risk factor for metabolic complications but has not been fully evaluated in prediction model for GDM. In the current study, we aimed to develop early prediction model for GDM using machine learning-based methods and to evaluate whether addition of NAFLD-associated variables would increase the model performance. Study Design: This is a secondary analysis from the prospective NAFLD pregnancy cohort. Pregnant women were evaluated for NAFLD at 10-14 weeks and was screened for GDM at 24-28 weeks. The clinical variables before 14 weeks were used to develop prediction model for GDM in four settings [Setting 1,2,3, and 4], and we also selected top 11 important variables using stepwise variable selection method [Setting 5] (Table). The study population was divided into training: test set (2:1 ratio) and the prediction model developed from training set was evaluated in the test set. Logistic regression, random forest, support vector machine, and deep neural network were used to construct the prediction model. Results: A total of 1443 women were included, and 86 women (5.96%) was diagnosed as GDM. The performance of prediction model was highest in [Setting 4] among Settings 1-4, demonstrating that the addition of NAFLD-associated variables improved the prediction model performance. In addition, the model with 11 important variables [Setting 5] showed similar accuracy with [Setting 4] (Table and Figure). Overall, we could suggest the final model derived from 11 important variables constructed by random forest algorithm. Conclusion: We developed early prediction model for GDM using machine learning-based method. The proposed model needs to be evaluated in further prospective studies. [Formula presented] [Formula presented]
