N2  - Objective: Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) affects 30% of the U.S. and is a risk factor for cirrhosis, liver cancer, and is the leading cause for liver transplant among women. Physiologic estrogen fluctuations and rapid weight change in pregnancy may play important roles in NAFLD. Data regarding NAFLD in pregnancy, and its association with metabolic disorders in pregnancy, is limited. Transient elastography (TE)(Fibroscan) with continued attenuation parameter (CAP) is a noninvasive way to diagnose steatosis and liver stiffness/fibrosis. Study Design: IRB approved prospective study in which TE and liver function tests were performed 1-2 days postpartum. CAP ≥300 was classified as significant steatosis. Elevated liver stiffness/fibrosis was defined as ≥7 kilopascals(KPA). Chi-square analysis was used to compare differences between groups. Univariate and bivariate logistic regression was used to determine significant predictors of elevated CAP and KPA. Results: 91 patients were included: 20(22%) had gestational diabetes (GDM), 13(14%) had gestational hypertension (gHTN), and 15(17%) had preeclampsia (PEC). Women with these metabolic disorders of pregnancy were more likely to be Black, and undergo C-section, but had lower median weight gain (Table 1). Women with KPA≥7 were more likely to have ALT≥25, Type 2 diabetes, and PEC(p<0.05)(Table 2). Pre- and post-gravid BMI, and GDM were not associated with increased KPA. Overweight pregravid BMI, and chronic hypertension were associated with CAP≥300(p<0.05). GDM, PEC, and gHTN were not associated with CAP≥300. In logistic regression analysis, PEC(OR 4.32,p=0.033) and ALT>25(OR of 5.59,p=0.007) were associated with KPA≥7 independent of BMI. Conclusion: Women with preeclampsia and elevated ALT were more likely to have elevated postpartum liver stiffness. Pregravid BMI≥25 and ≥30 were associated with increased liver steatosis, although did not appear to impact liver stiffness. Consideration should be made for screening women with PEC and obesity for liver disease during pregnancy with potential for lifestyle intervention. Implementation of TE postpartum is feasible. [Formula presented] [Formula presented]
