N2  - Background: There has been an increasing recognition of liver disease in women of childbearing age but liver tests are not routinely recommended during pregnancy care Elevated ALT is considered abnormal in pregnancy We prospectively evaluated consecutive samples from pregnant women presenting to labor and delivery (L&D) for ALT <25, ≥25, ≥50, ≥125, and ≥250 U/L. Methods: ALT was measured on discarded blood samples of consecutive L&D admissions both antepartum and at time of delivery at a high-volume obstetrics hospital in New York City over a 2 month period Patient characteristics including presence of chronic liver disease and pregnancy-specific liver disease between patients with normal and abnormal ALT were evaluated Predictors of elevated ALT in women without known liver disease were evaluated using logistic regression Results: Samples were collected from 996 patients; 1024 ALT values recorded. 131 (13.2%) had a peak ALT ≥ 25 IU/L; 20 (2%) had ALT ≥ 50 IU/L; 6 (0.6%) had ALT >125, and 3 (0.3%) had ALT >250. Among women with ALT ≥ 25, 105/131 (80%) were tested at delivery (with 13 (10%) during preterm delivery) and 26 (20%) from women tested for an antepartum admission Among women with ALT ≥ 25 identified in the study, 61/131 (47%) had not been tested during routine prenatal care; 20 (15%) had pre-eclampsia, 1 (0 8%) had hepatitis C, 5 (4%) had intrahepatic cholestasis (ICP) of pregnancy, 9 (7%) had gestational diabetes; there were no women with diagnosed nonalcoholic fatty liver disease Maternal age, race, ethnicity, marital status, and insurance status, gestational diabetes status were similar between ALT≥25 and ALT< 25 groups. Among women with ALT 50-124, 125-249, and >250, 29%, 100%, and 67% had liver disease diagnoses. Excluding those with a known diagnosis of liver disease, 107/910 (11 8%) had an ALT ≥ 25, with similar demographic features to women with ALT<25 (Table 1) In women without diagnosed liver disease, there was a trend towards an association of ALT ≥ 25 with every 5 pound pregnancy weight gain (OR 1 23; 95% CI 0 97, 1 55), despite no association with pre-pregnancy BMI (OR 1 00; 95% CI 0 95, 1 06) There was no association with race, age, ethnicity, or timing of delivery Conclusion: We identified a higher than previously reported prevalence of abnormal ALT in pregnancy (13 2%) Only 20% of women with abnormal ALT were associated with a recognized etiology (i e viral hepatitis, ICP, PE), however, the majority of women with elevated ALT did not have any recognized liver-related diagnosis, or have liver tests checked during routine care Among those without diagnosed liver disease, pregnancy weight gain trended towards association with ALT ≥ 25 IU/L, suggesting steatosis during pregnancy as a potential contributor to abnormal ALT Further evaluation of the role of ALT testing as part of routine prenatal care is needed, particularly in establishing baseline prevalence of liver test abnormalities during pregnancy. (Table Presented).
