N2  - Background: Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) affects ∼15-17% of U S women of reproductive age, with prevalence rising, and is the leading indication for liver transplant in women Prevalence and associated risk factors for NAFLD during pregnancy are not well defined, although a recent retrospective study suggested an association with adverse pregnancy outcomes We sought to prospectively characterize the prevalence and associated risk factors of NAFLD among pregnant women at a high-volume obstetrics center Methods: Consecutive pregnant women were consented for liver ultrasound (US) at the time of routine prenatal anatomy scan, typically done at 18-22 weeks' gestation Liver US performed by trained obstetric sonographers were reviewed for grading of hepatic steatosis by a blinded radiologist We compared demographic and clinical characteristics between those with and without steatosis on US and performed univariable and multivariable logistic regression to determine predictors of steatosis US screening was compared to other lab-based NAFLD scoring systems including Hepatic Steatosis Index (HSI), NAFLD fibrosis score (NFS), and FIB-4 using baseline labs (1-year pre-pregnancy through 1st trimester) Results: We obtained satisfactory liver US on 383 patients in 4 months Median time to obtain images was 41 seconds Median age was 28 (IQR 9), 219 (58%) were Hispanic, 162 (42%) nulliparous 53 (14%) had steatosis on US (47 grade 1, 4 grade 2, 2 grade 3); 18% of Hispanic patients had steatosis 86% of those with steatosis on US had HSI≥36 vs. 54% without steatosis (p<0.01). There were no significant differences in NAFLD fibrosis score or FIB-4 between those with and without steatosis Of those with steatosis, 1 had a previous diagnosis of NAFLD Patients with steatosis had higher median BMI (29 1 vs 26 6, p<0 01) and were more likely to be Hispanic (75 5% vs 55 4%, p<0 01) They were also more likely to have chronic hypertension (17% vs 6 5%, p<0 01), type 2 diabetes (DM2) (7 6% vs 2 1%, p=0 03), autoimmune thyroid disease (13 2% vs 1 5%, p<0 01) and pregnancy history of gestational diabetes (23 1% vs 6 2%, p=0 01 ) On multivariable regression, DM2, Hispanic ethnicity, BMI ≥25, and autoimmune thyroid disease were associated with steatosis (Table 1) Conclusion: NAFLD prevalence among pregnant women in this prospective USbased study was 14%, similar to reports in non-pregnant women of reproductive age Most had no prior diagnosis of NAFLD, highlighting a unique opportunity to identify and link women to specialized care Integrating liver US into obstetric care was feasible and effective in identifying women with NAFLD There should be higher suspicion for undiagnosed NAFLD particularly among Hispanic women and those with BMI≥25, DM2 or thyroid disease. Additional research should be done to explore longitudinal changes in NAFLD diagnosis and severity during pregnancy and influence on postpartum outcomes.
