N2  - Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is closely associated with the metabolic syndrome and will be renamed metabolic-dysfunction-associated fatty liver disease (MAFLD) according to an expert recommendation. MAFLD is the most common liver disease in Europe with a prevalence of 25% for the general population and over 50% in patients with diabetes mellitus type 2 (T2DM). In recent years, genetic association studies have identified a number of gene variants that predispose to the development and progression of this disease. It is assumed that approximately 50% of the observed interindividual variation in liver fat content is determined by genetic factors. Of particular importance are the risk variants in the genes PNPLA3 (patatin-like phospholipase-containing 3), TM6SF2 (transmembrane 6 superfamily member 2), MBOAT7 (membrane bound O‑acyltransferase domain containing 7), GCKR (glucokinase regulator), and HSD17B13 (hydroxysteroid 17-beta-dehydrogenase 13). The risk for the development of steatosis, steatohepatitis, liver cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma mediated by these genetic variants has an additive effect. Whether knowledge of these risk variants will translate into an individualized surveillance and therapeutic strategy will have to be determined in the future. The overview article also summarizes pathophysiological effects of the risk gene variants in relation to other aspects of the metabolic syndrome such as T2DM and hyperlipidemia. The effect of the risk gene variants on metabolism increases with increasing body weight and underscores the mutual influence of MAFLD, T2DM, hyperlipidemia, and obesity.
