N2  - Background: The incidence of cirrhosis is increasing in young women, which may have implications on reproductive health. It is unclear whether the rate of pregnancy with cirrhosis is rising or whether cirrhosis confers more serious maternal and fetal risks. Methods: Using weighted data from the United States National Inpatient Sample (NIS), we evaluated temporal trends of pregnancy with cirrhosis from 2007-2016. Data from 2012-2016 (due to a change in hospital sampling in 2012) was used to determine the association of cirrhosis with maternal and fetal outcomes as compared to pregnancies with non-cirrhotic chronic liver disease (CLD) or no CLD. Diagnoses were captured from discharge records from time of delivery event using ICD-9 and 10 codes. Pregnancies with cirrhosis were estimated by calendar year and temporal trends tested by linear regression. The association of cirrhosis with study outcomes was analyzed by logistic regression accounting for NIS sample design, adjusted for age, race, multiple gestation, and pre-pregnancy diabetes, hypertension, dyslipidemia, and obesity. Results: The rate of pregnancies with cirrhosis more than doubled from 2.5/100,000 in 2007 to 6.6/100,000 in 2016 (p=0.02) (FIGURE). There were 18,573,070 deliveries from 2012-2016; 915 had cirrhosis (mean age 32, 62% white), 120,185 had non-cirrhotic CLD (mean age 30, 57% white), and 18,451,970 with no CLD (mean age 29, 54% white). The most common etiologies of cirrhosis were viral hepatitis (51%), autoimmune (21%), other/mixed (14%), and non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (8%). Hepatic decompensation (i.e. variceal hemorrhage, ascites, hepatic encephalopathy) occurred in 165/915 (18%) of pregnancies with cirrhosis. Gestational hypertension (12% vs 4%, p=0.03) and post-partum hemorrhage (27% vs 7%, p<0.001) were more common among pregnancies with decompensated vs compensated cirrhosis. Compared to non-cirrhotic CLD or no CLD, pregnancies with cirrhosis had more hypertensive complications (pre-eclampsia, eclampsia, or hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelet syndrome) (16% vs 4%), cesarean section (55% vs 33-37%), post-partum hemorrhage (11% vs 3-5%), and maternal death (1% vs 0.01-0.03%), p values <0.001. Pregnancies with cirrhosis also had more gestational diabetes (13% vs 7-9%, p=0.07), pre-term birth (16% vs 5-7%, p<0.001), and fetal death (3% vs 1%, p=0.001) vs other groups. On adjusted analysis, cirrhosis remained associated with notable maternal and fetal risks, including hypertensive complications, post-partum hemorrhage, and pre-term birth (TABLE). Conclusions: The rate of cirrhosis in pregnancy has more than doubled over the past 10 years. While maternal death is uncommon, other serious maternal and fetal risks are higher. Portal hypertension should be optimally managed prior to conception, with subsequent multidisciplinary management throughout pregnancy.
