N2  - Background: Cirrhosis and incident NAFLD are both rising in young adults, which may affect women of childbearing age Trends in NAFLD cirrhosis during pregnancy have not been reported, and it is not known whether NAFLD cirrhosis in pregnancy confers worse maternal-fetal outcomes as compared to other causes of cirrhosis Methods: Populationbased retrospective cohort study from Ontario, Canada using universal administrative healthcare data from 01/01/2000- 12/31/2016 This study includes women ages 18-44 years with singleton pregnancies through at least 20 weeks of gestation, and with a cirrhosis diagnosis identified by a validated cirrhosis case definition (sensitivity 98%, specificity 77%). Mother-infant record linkage included data through 3-months of post-partum follow-up Maternal and fetal outcomes were obtained from administrative codes and the linked motherinfant dataset Outcomes were compared between mothers with NAFLD vs non-NAFLD causes of cirrhosis Results: 4,391 pregnant women with cirrhosis were included; median age 31 years (IQR 26-34); n=1,806 with NAFLD and n=2,585 with non-NAFLD causes of cirrhosis The number of pregnant women with NAFLD cirrhosis more than tripled from 2000 to 2016 (from 50 per year in 2000 to 177 per year by 2016, p<0 001), with fairly stable numbers in non-NAFLD cirrhosis (88 per year in 2000 to 94 per year in 2016) By the end of the study period, NAFLD accounted for 65% of all cirrhosis cases in pregnancy Regarding maternal complications, postpartum hemorrhage was numerically less common while pre-eclampsia/eclampsia and gestational hypertension were more common in women with NAFLD vs non-NAFLD cirrhosis (TABLE) NAFLD cirrhosis was not associated with more c-sections or gestational diabetes as compared to non-NAFLD cirrhosis Regarding fetal outcomes, NAFLD cirrhosis was associated with less stillbirth, pre-term delivery, and small for gestational age, although was associated with large for gestational age infants (13 5% vs 8 4%, p<0 001) (TABLE) Conclusion: The number of pregnant women with NAFLD cirrhosis has nearly tripled over the past 15 years and NAFLD is now by far the most common etiology of cirrhosis in pregnant women Compared to other causes of cirrhosis, NAFLD was not associated with worse maternal outcomes, though was associated with large for gestational age infants, which does confer increased risks to infant health.
