N2  - Background: Liver failure in pregnancy is rare and life-threatening complication. It is usually caused by HELLP (haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes and low platelets) syndrome leading to liver haematomas or rupture, AFLP (acute fatty liver of pregnancy) or liver infarction. Other aetiologies include hepatitis, toxins and Budd-Chiari syndrome, as coincidental occurrence is possible. Aetiologies may be overlapping and present a diagnostic challenge to the clinicians. Method: xxxxxxxxxxx Results: We present a case report of 32-year- old woman of West-African origin, who was previously healthy but had developed gestational diabetes. She had vaginal delivery at 40 weeks of gestation. Towards the end of the delivery she got progressively hypertensive. Roughly an hour after giving birth, she had an eclamptic seizure and was taken to intensive care unit (ICU). She developed signs of HELLP, disseminated intravascular coagulation and rapidly declining liver function. As the days went by, ultrasound studies showed her liver partly necrotic, but with some portal flow. Her condition deteriorated to a stage where MARS® (Molecular Adsorbent Recirculating System) had to be instituted. Her liver function did not show signs of improving and on the 9th day after delivery she underwent orthotopic liver transplantation. Recovery was gradual, she was discharged from the ICU on 10th day after the transplantation. Conclusion: Pathologists examination showed mostly necrotic liver tissue with some haemorrhagic areas. Preserved hepatocytes had steatosis and thrombus formation was discovered in veins as well. Pathological diagnosis was not definitive. Serological studies ruled out infective aetiology. There was no clear diagnosis, but the conclusion amongst clinicians was that this was a case of severe HELLP. The case represents the difficulty in definitive diagnosis when clinical picture is not like a text book case.
