N2  - Objectives: Metabolism of over-weight individuals could be exposed to risk of chronic low-grade pro-inflammatory effect and oxidative stress. New biomarkers of inflammatory and oxidative and antioxidant status have been studied recently. Total oxidant status (TOS), total antioxidant status (TAS) and inflammatory parameters can be clinical and diagnostic tools of medical condition such as osteoporosis, ankylosing spondylitis ,preterm rupture of membranes, neonatal diseases,Hashimoto's thyroiditis, psychological disorders,effects of exercise,chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, gestational diabetes mellitus ,fatty liver disease, laparoscopic procedures, accompanying inflammatory condition like diabetic neuropathy, chronic hepatitis. Materials-Methods: In study, the over -weight group was 50 adults but otherwise healthy then other group age and sex-matched adults of normal-weight group was formed. For search, groups have been comprised of two groups with inflammatory biomarkers, TOS-TAS levels. The oxidative stress index (OSI) was computed as a marker of the grade of oxidative stress. Results: The over-weight group displayed higher levels of fasting glucose, insulin resistance, body mass index. Also, Higher TOS as well as CRP is related to the group and lower TAS than the normal-weight group is shown (p<0.05). CRP levels in plasma were positively correlated with insulin and glucose levels. (p<0.05). In addition, there was a significant relationship between OSI and insulin resistance in the over-weight group. (p<0.05) Conclusions: The coexistence of insulin resistance and free fatty acid output can cause proinflammatory cytokines and cellular stress including oxidative and endoplasmic reticulum stress. As well as a raise over weight-related subclinical inflammation (the risk of long-term vascular damage) and a fall antioxidant capacity is significant. Thus, Inflammatory TOS and TAS biomarkers can be used significant clinical and diagnostic tools of medical purposes.
