N2  - Liver enzymes are markers of liver fat deposits and may play a role in glucose homeostasis, but their individual roles during pregnancy and interaction with abdominal adiposity in relation to subsequent risk of GDM remain unclear. We prospectively investigated alanine aminotransferase (ALT), γ-glutamyl transferase (GGT) and waist-to-hip-ratio (WHR) in relation to GDM risk in a nested case-control study within the Pregnancy Environment and Lifestyle Study. Among 815 women who had delivered by June 2015, 78 GDM cases were diagnosed at 24-28 weeks of gestation and each was matched to two controls on age, race/ethnicity and gestational weeks at blood draw. Serum ALT and GGT were measured at visit 1 (V1, 10-13 gestational weeks) and visit 2 (V2, 16-19 gestational weeks). Waist and hip circumferences were measured at V1. Compared to controls, GDM cases had higher levels of ALT and GGT at both V1 and V2 and higher WHR (all P<0.05). After adjustment for parity, family history of diabetes and alcohol consumption, GGT but not ALT in the highest vs. lowest quartile at V1 and V2 was associated with a 3.01-fold (95% CI 1.23-7.38) and 3.06-fold (1.18-7.94) increased GDM risk, respectively; however, the associations were attenuated and became non-significant after additional adjustment for body mass index (BMI) and WHR. After adjustment for parity, family history of diabetes, alcohol consumption and BMI, WHR in the highest vs. lowest quartile was associated with a 7.05-fold (1.8-27.5) increased risk of GDM. Furthermore, having an ALT or GGT and a WHR above the respective median was associated with 4-fold (1.55-12.7) and 8-fold (2.29-27.8) increased GDM risk compared to women with both values below the median at V1; results were similar at V2. In contrast, no joint effects between liver enzymes and BMI were observed. Our findings suggest an adverse synergistic effect of elevated liver enzymes and abdominal adiposity as early as the first trimester on subsequent risk of GDM, which may help to early identify high-risk women.
