N2  - OBJECTIVE: Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease(NAFLD) is now considered as a hepatic manifestation of metabolic syndrome and elevated alanine aminotransferase(ALT) is commonly related to NAFLD in the absence of viral hepatitis or alcohol abuse. Previous studies have indicated that elevated ALT is associated with diabetes or insulin resistance in adults, but the clinical significance of ALTor NAFLD in pregnancy has not been well determined. The objective of this study was to determine the association between elevated ALT in early pregnancy and the development of gestational diabetes (GDM) or preeclampsia in late pregnancy. STUDY DESIGN: In this retrospective cohort study, pregnant women who met the following inclusion criteria were included: 1) singleton pregnancy; 2) ALT measured at 4-20 weeks of gestation; 3) screened for GDM and delivered in Cheil General Hospital and Women's Healthcare Center. Cases with viral hepatitis or other liver diseases were excluded. The early ALT levels were divided into two groups (normal ALT (≥95percentile) and elevated ALT (>95 percentile)), and the frequency of GDM and preeclampsia was compared between the two groups of cases. GDM was screened and diagnosed by twostep procedure (50g oral glucose challenge test followed by 75g glucose challenge test). RESULTS: A total of 2224 women met the inclusion criteria. The frequency of GDM and preeclampsia were 2.4% (by WHO criteria) and 1.2%, respectively. Cases with elevated early AST levels(> 95percentile) had higher risk of subsequent GDM or preeclampsia (GDM by WHO criteria, 2.1% in normal ALT vs. 7.4% in elevated ALT, p<0.005; preeclampsia, 1.0% in normal ALT vs. 4.1% in elevated AST, p<0.05). This relationship between elevated ALT and increased risk of GDM /pree clampsia remained significant after adjustment for maternal age and pre-pregnancy BMI. CONCLUSION: Elevated unexplained ALT in early pregnancy was associated with the risk of subsequent development of GDM and preeclampsia in late pregnancy. (Table Presented).
