N2  - Prior gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is associated with an increased risk of nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD). This study evaluates the effect of one-year treatment with liraglutide on NAFLD in nondiabetic women with prior GDM. The study includes a subgroup of patients from a randomised, double-blinded, investigator-initiated, placebo-controlled trial examining nondiabetic women with prior GDM for whom we have full data set (n=82, age (mean±standard deviation): 38±5 years, body weight: 88±16 kg, BMI: 32±5 kg/m2) allocated to liraglutide 1.8 mg subcutaneously once daily (n=37) or placebo (n=45). All women underwent abdominal ultrasound to diagnose NAFLD at baseline and after one year and a subgroup (n=60) also underwent transient elastography (FibroScan®) with assessment of intrahepatic fat by controlled attenuation parameter (CAP) both at baseline and after one year (CAP: 266±48 dB/m). None had elevated liver blood tests or cirrhosis at baseline. Based on the abdominal ultrasound and elastography, 18 women had NAFLD at baseline. Eleven women (13%) developed NAFLD during the one-year follow-up. Eight women had NAFLD at baseline, but not after one year. These women had lower alanine aminotransferase levels at the end of follow-up compared to baseline (P=0.042). None of the remaining patient characteristics were associated with resolution of NAFLD. Liraglutide had no effect on the prevention, development or resolution of NAFLD (NS), but significantly reduced intrahepatic fat assessed by CAP compared to placebo (-35±52 vs. -5±52 dB/m, P=0.003). Liraglutide reduced bodyweight (4.7±5.2 vs. 1.4±5.5 kg, P=0.007), but this effect was not associated with CAP values or resolution of NAFLD in multivariable analyses. This one-year trial found no effect of liraglutide on presence of NAFLD but a significant weight loss and reduced steatosis as measured by CAP was observed.
