N2  - There is a well-established association of elevated alanine aminotransferase, attributed to non-alcoholic fatty liver disease, with later development of type 2 diabetes mellitus among non-pregnant adults. We therefore sought to determine whether ALT values in early pregnancy were associated with development of gestational diabetes (GDM). We performed a nested case-control study utilizing prospectively banked serum samples collected between 8-18 weeks gestation. We excluded women with known diabetes, liver disease, or moderate self-reported alcohol use during pregnancy and documented by medical record review. We included 83 cases who were later diagnosed with GDM and 247 controls matched for pre-pregnancy BMI and with a 3rd trimester 50-g GLT result ≤120mg/dL. We compared early ALT values in cases with controls. We then performed a conditional logistic regression to model the adjusted odds of GDM in women with elevated ALT, stratified by first trimester BMI. Cases, women who were later diagnosed with GDM, were older (mean age±SD 34±5 vs. 32±6 years, p=0.001) and more likely to be of Asian ethnicity (16.9% vs. 2.8%, p=0.03) than controls, but otherwise were similar. The median (IQR) ALT in cases was 15 (12, 19) IU/L compared to 13 (11, 18) IU/L in women with normal glucose tolerance (p=0.07). Among non-obese women (first trimester BMI <30kg/m2), ALT above the 75% ile (19 IU/L) was associated with a four-fold increased risk of GDM (adjusted OR 4.56 [1.45, 14.27]), while there was no association (aOR 0.36 [0.11, 1.20]) among obese women. Similarly, each unit increase in log-transformed ALT was associated with a threefold increased risk of GDM in non-obese (aOR 3.15 [1.04, 9.54]) but not in obese (aOR 0.97 [0.30, 3.15]) women. Elevated ALT early in pregnancy was associated with increased risk for GDM in non-obese women. Elevated ALT may be a useful indicator of GDM risk in a population currently considered low-risk for GDM.
