N2  - Background: Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is the most prevalent form of liver disease in the United States and an increasingly common cause of cirrhosis. Identifying patients with an increased risk for NAFLD at an early age may allow interventions to occur that would prevent NAFLD development or progression. Insulin resistance is associated with NAFLD and gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is an early indicator of insulin resistance. We hypothesized that a history of GDM would identify women at higher risk of NAFLD. Methods: Participants from the multicenter Coronary Artery Risk Development in Young Adults (CARDIA) cohort study who delivered ≥ 1 birth, were free of diabetes prior to pregnancy(ies), and underwent CT quantification of hepatic steatosis 25 years following cohort entry (Y25: 2010-2011) were included (n = 1115). History of GDM was defined by self-report and collected prospectively for births during the 25-year study period; the accuracy of self-report has been previously validated by review of prenatal glucose tolerance tests in a subset of this cohort. NAFLD was assessed by central review of CT images and defined by liver attenuation (LA) ≤ 40 Hounsfield Units after exclusion of other causes of hepatic steatosis. LA was the average of nine measurements on three CT slices of the right lobe of the liver. Univariate and multivariate logistic regression analyses were performed; p value < 0.05 was the criterion used for variable inclusion. Results: Of 1,115 women meeting entry criteria (57% black, 43% white, mean age at 25-year mark 50 yrs), 124 (11%) reported a history of GDM and 75 (7%) met CT definition for NAFLD at year 25. At baseline, compared to women without a history of GDM, those who had or developed GDM over the study period had higher mean body mass index (BMI) (25.8 vs. 24.4 kg/m2, p<0.01), homeostatic model assessment of insulin resistance (HOMA-IR) (2.0 vs. 1.6, p<0.01), fasting plasma triglycerides (74 vs. 64 mg/dL, p<0.01) and waist circumference (77 vs. 74 cm, p<0.01). History of GDM was associated with NAFLD at the 25-year visit (14% vs. 5.8% in those without GDM, OR: 2.56, 95% CI: 1.44-4.55, p<0.01). History of GDM remained a strong predictor of NAFLD (OR: 2.29, 95% CI: 1.23-4.27, p=0.01) after adjustment for covariates (Table 1). In a multivariable model that adjusted for DM at year 25, the association between history of GDM and NAFLD was stronger (OR: 1.99, 95% CI: 1.06-3.74, p=0.03) than betweenDMat year 25 and NAFLD (OR: 1.50, 95% CI: 1.10-2.04, p=0.01). Conclusion: GDM is an independent risk marker for NAFLD and represents an opportunity to identify those at risk for NAFLD at a young age. History of GDM may be a better marker of NAFLD risk than traditional risk markers for NAFLD in middle age including DM. (Table Presented).
