N2  - INTRODUCTION: Nonalcoholic fatty liver reflects hepatic insulin resistance and is a key feature of metabolic syndrome. There is little known about the impact of maternal nonalcoholic fatty liver disease on fetal growth. The purpose of this study was therefore to investigate the association of early elevated ALT with large for gestational age birthweight. METHODS: This is a secondary analysis from a nested case-control study of ALT values measured between 8-18 weeks and subsequent gestational diabetes (GDM). We included women with singleton gestations and without known diabetes, liver disease or moderate self-reported alcohol use during pregnancy. We used inverse probability weighting to standardize the population and minimize selection bias. We calculated population-based birthweight z scores and defined LGA as at or above the 90%ile. We compared baseline characteristics with either ANOVA, Fisher's exact test or Wilcoxon rank sum. Covariates were identified by univariate regression analysis. We then performed conditional logistic regression to evaluate the relationship between either ALT≥90%ile or logALT and LGA adjusting for maternal age, BMI, parity, GDM, smoking and maternal weight gain. RESULTS: We identified 26 cases of LGA out of 323 subjects. The mean(SD) maternal BMI was higher in the LGA group compared to controls (33.7[4.3] vs 28.9[6.5] p=0.002) and LGA babies were less likely to be male (8[31%] vs 172[58%] p=0.01) and had a higher mean (SD) gestational age (39.5w[0.9] vs 38.4[2.3] p=0.01). Maternal and infant characteristics were otherwise similar. The geometric mean (95%CI) ALT among the LGA cases was 20.3U/L (14.7,28.1) compared to 14.2U/L(13.4,14.9) for controls. ALT ≥90%ile (26U/L) was associated with a four-fold increased odds of LGA (adjusted OR 4.03 [2.84,5.70] p<0.0001). Each unit increase in log-transformed ALT was associated with a three-fold odds of LGA (aOR 3.05 [2.27,4.10] p<0.0001). The association remained when analysis was restricted to women without GDM (ALT≥90%ile aOR 4.21 [1.20,14.82] p=0.03; logALT aOR 3.05 [1.04,8.96] p=0.04). CONCLUSIONS: Unexplained elevated ALT in the first trimester is associated with a four-fold increase in the odds of LGA even in the absence of clinical glucose intolerance. We hypothesize this may represent the impact of maternal hepatic insulin resistance on the fetal developmental milieu.
