N2  - Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) increases the risk of developing type 2 diabetes later in life. Type 2 diabetes and obesity are associated with nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD). We examined glucose tolerance and fat distribution in obese, non-diabetic women with prior GDM. Based on a 75g-oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT), non-diabetic women with previous GDM were classified as having normal glucose tolerance (NGT) or prediabetes (impaired fasting glucose and/or impaired glucose tolerance). Insulin resistance (IR) was assessed by the homeostasis model assessment (HOMAIR). All women underwent an ultrasound scan of the liver, blood sampling, and a dual energy X-ray absorptiometry (DXA) scan. The android-to-gynoid fat-ratio and total visceral fat mass were calculated based on the DXA scan. A questionnaire was used to evaluate alcohol consumption habits. Women with previous GDM were included (n=59; age: 38±5 years (mean±standard deviation); BMI: 31±5 kg/m2; HbA1c: 33±5 mmol/mol; HOMAIR: 1.2±0.6; duration since GDM: 5±2 years). Eighteen women had NGT (fasting plasma glucose (FPG) 5.4±0.3 mM; 2h-OGTT plasma glucose (PG): 7.2±1.0 mM) and 41 women had prediabetes (FPG 5.5±0.5 mM; 2h-OGTT PG: 9.3±1.1 mM). NAFLD was diagnosed in 5 (28%) women with NGT and 14 (34%) with prediabetes). Glucose tolerance status (NGT or prediabetes) did not coincide with NAFLD (P=0.63). Multivariable logistic regression analyses showed that NAFLD was positively associated with IR level (P=0.03), BMI (P=0.004), C-peptide (P=0.028), mass of visceral fat (P=0.008), the androidto- gynoid fat-ratio (P=0.03), and plasma levels of alanine amino transferase (P=0.01) and aspartate amino transferase (P=0.03). NAFLD is prevalent in women with previous GDM regardless of their glucose tolerance status, but seems to be associated with IR, high BMI, high C-peptide levels, android fat distribution, and elevated plasma liver enzymes.
