N2  - Background Polyuria is a common pregnancy symptom but excessive polyuria may be associated with gestational diabetes insipidus (GDI). GDI is a rare form of the endocrine disorder characterised by polyuria, nocturia and polydipsia due to inadequate or absent antidiuretic hormone. It is associated with pre-eclampsia and acute fatty liver disease. We discuss clinical presentation, diagnosis and management in two cases of GDI who delivered in our district hospital. Case 1 Thirty-six-year-old primigravida presented with vomiting, polydipsia and polyuria at 35 weeks of gestation. She was treated for suspected urinary tract infection and dehydration. Hypernatraemia was detected and diagnosis of GDI was then confirmed after water deprivation test. Her symptoms subsided with commencement on desmospressin. Postpartum normal urine and serum electrolyte balance concluded GDI. Case 2 Thirty-five-year-old primigravida developed recurrent GDI in her second pregnancy from 32 weeks of gestation and was promptly treated with desmopressin. Three years ago in her first pregnancy, she complained of polyuria, polydipsia and nocturia from 34 weeks of gestation. A normal glucose tolerance test ruled out gestational diabetes mellitus. Three weeks into her symptoms, GDI was suspected and she was started on desmopressin. She developed severe pre-eclampsia during labour and was treated with magnesium sulphate and intravenous labetalol. Urine osmolality performed 1 month postnatally was normal confirming GDI. Conclusion Our cases illustrate how patients with GDI are often diagnosed late as early symptoms mimic normal pregnancy and other medical conditions. Prompt diagnosis allows early intervention and prevention of serious maternal and fetal complications.
