N2  - Description of case: Diabetes insipidus (DI) is an uncommon complication of acute fatty liver of pregnancy (AFLP). AFLP is rare with an approximate incidence of 1 in 7000 to 1 in 20,000 deliveries.1 There can be serious maternal and neonatal outcomes if DI goes unrecognised. An understanding of the physiological mechanisms of sodium and water balance in pregnancy is important for the effective diagnosis and management of this condition. During pregnancy, there is a β-hCG mediated fall in the hypothalamic set point for osmolality by between 5-10osm/kg. This change in osmostat is thought to be maintained by a lowering of the AVP secretion threshold and is associated with a lowering of the serum concentration of sodium by up to 5mmol/L. In addition, there is increased AVP degradation due to the production of placental vasopressinases, which are metabolised by the liver. Diabetes insipidus occurs in AFLP when there is an increased breakdown of AVP due to reduced breakdown of vasopressinase by the liver.2 Management includes careful fl uid balance and treatment with desmopressin which is not degraded by vasopressinases. Urgent delivery may be indicated. We describe a case series of four patients with AFLP complicated by diabetes insipidus which illustrate the range of clinical presentations of this condition, its treatment and neonatal outcomes. This case series also highlights the changes in physiological osmoregulatory mechanisms that occur in pregnancy. //////////1. Knight M, et al. A prospective national study of acute fatty liver of pregnancy in the UK Gut 2008; 57: 951-6. 2. Aleksandrov N, et al. Gestational diabetes insipidus: A review of an underdiagnosed condition. JOGC 2010; 32: 225-31. Issues of interest in the case 1. Diabetes insipidus in an uncommon complication of acute fatty liver of pregnancy. 2. There is a β-hCG mediated fall in the hypothalamic set point for osmolality by between 5-10osm/kg. 3. Plasma sodium decreases by 5mmol/L in pregnancy. 4. Diabetes insipidus occurs in AFLP when there is an increased breakdown of AVP due to reduced breakdown of vasopressinase by the liver.
