N2  - Gestational diabetes and fatty liver are health risks for both mother and offspring. Dietary fructose is linked to hepatic steatosis and insulin resistance. We investigated the effects of fructose intake on hepatic steatosis and insulin resistance in pregnant and lactating rats and compared responses to a low protein diet, an established maternal dietary insult that predisposes offspring to risks for metabolic syndrome. Sprague-Dawley rats (n=25) were fed either a control diet (C), a 63% fructose diet (F) or an 8% protein diet (LP) during pregnancy and lactation. Glucose tolerance test at day 17 of pregnancy revealed greater (P < 0.1) blood glucose at 10 (75.6 vs 64.0±2.5 mg/dl) and 20 (72.4 vs 58.6±1.7 mg/dl) min after glucose dose and greater area under the curve (4302.3 vs 3763.4±117.0 mg·dl-1·min) for F fed rats compared to C fed rats. The dams were sacrificed at 21 days postpartum. The F fed rats had enlarged (P < 0.05) livers (11.4, vs 4.8±0.6 % body weight). Both F and LP fed rats had elevated (P < 0.05) liver triglyceride (199.6, 130.0 vs 19.9±2.9 mg/g liver weight) compared to C fed rats. Fructose induced fatty liver and glucose intolerance in pregnant and lactating rats. Low protein induced fatty liver intermediate to F and C diets. The data demonstrate gestational diabetes coupled with hepatic steatosis in fructose fed dams, which is more severe than a fetal programming diet of low protein.
